      "greedy_continuation": " A steam locomotive is a large engine that is powered by steam. Steam is a gas that is made by burning wood. When the engine is running, the steam is turned into a high-pressure stream that pushes the wheels. When the steam is turned off, the wheels stop turning and the train",
      "samples": [
        " The steam engine is a giant cylinder of compressed air that pushes against a piston inside. The force of the steam is the same force that pushes the piston into the cylinder. On the back of the piston, there is a piston rod, which passes through the cylinder and drives the wheels. The farther away",
        " The engine is a small box with a slide valve and a piston in it. As the piston moves, the slide valve closes and the steam is discharged from the piston. The first time the valve opened, the piston moved fast and the steam pushed the wheels forward so that the locomotive was moving quickly",
        " When the steam's pistons are released, the train is allowed to travel at high speed without slowing down or losing speed.\n\nQ: What is the origin of the term, “water wag?”\nA: The New England settlers brought with them the method of “wag” to"
