    {
      "id": "L2-human_anatomy-1",
      "level": "L2",
      "topic": "human anatomy",
      "prompt": "Short explanations, written in plain language.\n\nQ: Explain in your own words why a bicycle stays upright while it is moving.\nA: A moving bicycle keeps correcting itself. When it begins to lean to one side, the front wheel turns slightly into the lean, which steers the wheels back underneath the rider's weight. The faster it travels, the smaller and quicker those corrections are, so the bicycle feels steady.\n\nQ: Explain in your own words why a lighthouse can be seen from far out at sea.\nA: A lighthouse is built high up so that its beam clears the curve of the horizon. Lenses and mirrors gather the lamp's light and squeeze it into a narrow horizontal beam instead of letting it spread in every direction. Because the light is concentrated and starts from a height, a ship many miles away still receives enough of it to see the flash.\n\nQ: Explain in your own words how the lungs supply the body with oxygen.\nA:",
