      "greedy_continuation": " Yeast is a living organism that feeds on sugar. When it is added to the dough, it feeds on the sugar and converts it into carbon dioxide and water. The carbon dioxide and water rise to the surface of the dough, which causes the dough to rise.\n\nQ: Explain in",
      "samples": [
        " Yeast contains some sugar, which causes it to act as a food extractor. Air is drawn into the dough and the yeast spreads out on top of the dough. When the sugars are consumed, the yeast turns into a gas which pushes the air out from the dough. The dough rises.\n",
        " When yeast is added to flour, it combines with it and creates a gas, which bubbles up and rises up the dough. When sugar is added to the dough, it combines with the yeast to create carbon dioxide, which expands and rises up the dough. When baking bread, the dough keeps rising until",
        " Yeast rises because it brings in carbon dioxide, which makes the yeast grow. The yeast is concentrated in the middle and thins as it rises, so the outside of the dough is very porous. But the middle of the dough is very dense, so it can hold more of the gas.\n"
      ],
      "manipulation": {}
    },
