      "greedy_continuation": " Fish use their gills to breathe underwater because they need to move air in and out of their bodies. When they are swimming, they use their gills to move air in and out of their bodies. When they are resting, they use their gills to move air in and out of their bodies",
      "samples": [
        " Fish use their gills to breathe water as if they're swimming. They don't, of course, but the gills are an adaptation that helps them to stay submerged. The process of breathing in water by running water into their gills is called swimming.\n\nQ: Explain how a",
        " Fish breathe by moving through water, and their gills are used to suck water into the throat and lungs. That way, they can use a small amount of oxygen from the water, but without making any noise.\n\nQ: What is the first thing your son will do after he grows up",
        " Fish have hot and cold blood running through two special arteries on their sides. The blood from these arteries climbs the gills in the front, where the gills can be kept moist and cool, and then rises into the lungs in the back.\n\nQ: What is the first step of"
      ],
