      "topic": "classical music",
      "prompt": "Answers that combine two unrelated subjects, using one to explain the other.\n\nTask: Describe a barbershop organised as a court of law. Say which part of court procedure each step of the haircut copies and what it does for the customer.\nAnswer: The consultation is the arraignment: the customer states what he is charged with wanting and the barber reads it back to him, so there is no dispute later about what was agreed. The mirror on the far wall serves as the public gallery, since every cut is made in view and can be objected to while it is still reversible. Scissors and clippers act as opposing counsel, one arguing for length and the other for shape, and the barber alternates between them until neither has a further submission. Sweeping the floor is the sealing of the record: once the hair is gone the case cannot be reopened, and the customer settles costs on the way out.\n\nTask: Describe a village postal route as a knitting pattern. Say which knitting instruction each part of the route copies and what it does for the postman.\nAnswer: The main road is the cast-on row: it fixes how many houses the whole route will carry, and everything after it hangs from that count. Each side lane is a purl stitch worked in the opposite direction, so the postman doubles back and the fabric of the route lies flat instead of curling into wasted mileage. Houses that receive post only on certain days are slipped stitches, passed over without being worked but still counted, which keeps the rhythm of the walk even. The return to the sorting office is the cast-off, binding the loose end so that tomorrow's round can start from the same edge.\n\nTask: Describe how a musical round spreads through its voices as an outbreak spreads through a population, in 3-6 sentences. Each stage of the outbreak must correspond to a specific event in the music.\nAnswer:",
