      "greedy_continuation": " A chessboard is a board with a single square for each piece, and a single square for each pawn. The pieces are arranged in a line, and the pawns are arranged in a line, so the pieces and pawns are in a straight line. The pawns can move in any direction",
      "samples": [
        " Each player must capture a piece in a move that does not involve capturing. The problem with this rule is that it will be much easier to make your opponent give up his piece than to force him to do so, and it is hard to imagine a situation in which a legal capture would be anyway.",
        " Not every move is legal, so a player will never be sure of exactly what she might get, but in this chess variant the confusion gets worse when a player tries to predict where her opponent will move next, so the only way to win is to move very confidently. The rule is to have",
        " The only other chess variant we have is dead simple: the king always sacrifices a pawn. It's a longstanding puzzle that has prevented modern scholars from devising a new variant. But a new one can be easily made, as long as the structure is the same. The king always resigns his"
